268	// --- the delivery decision -------------------------------------------------------
269	
270	// How an `auto-merge` member's PR actually lands, given whether this repo has any
271	// pull_request-triggered workflow at all.
272	//
273	// GitHub's auto-merge is a QUEUE FOR CHECKS. On a repo with no PR CI there is
274	// nothing to queue behind: the mutation is rejected ("Pull request is in clean
275	// status"), and since the failure was swallowed the PR sat open forever — a repo
276	// that asked for `auto-merge` got no merge at all. Seven of twelve consumers were
277	// in exactly that shape (no `pull_request` trigger anywhere in .github/workflows).
278	//
279	// So: no CI to wait for → MERGE IT, which is what `auto-merge` meant all along.
280	//
281	// `hasPrCi` comes from ciDispatchPlan over the branch's own workflow files —
282	// `dispatch` (startable) plus `missing` (PR-triggered but not dispatchable). Both
283	// empty means no workflow anywhere runs on a pull_request, so no check will ever
284	// appear. That is a fact about the tree, not a race against checks registering.
285	//
286	// `gate` is what the BASE BRANCH requires (classifyMergeGate) — the thing
287	// GitHub's auto-merge actually queues behind. CI existing was the wrong
288	// predicate (#677): on an unprotected base the PR is mergeable the whole time
289	// its checks run, so `enablePullRequestAutoMerge` is rejected "clean status"
290	// every cycle no matter how green — arming there is doomed, and the delivery
291	// goes straight to verify-then-land instead. `unknown` arms: a gate we could
292	// not see is never merged past.
293	export function deliveryAction({ delivery, hasPrCi, gate = 'unknown' }) {
294	  if (delivery !== 'auto-merge') return 'none';
295	  if (!hasPrCi) return 'merge';
296	  return gate === 'absent' ? 'land' : 'arm';
297	}
