465	// --- landing THIS run's PR when the arm could not (#649) ----------------------
466	// On a member whose pull_request run is gated, EVERY arm fails; deferring to the
467	// next cycle's disposal put a standing ~24h offset between a task's output and
468	// that member's main. The evidence that settles it does not take a day to
469	// arrive: the dispatched run this delivery just started concludes in minutes at
470	// worst. So we wait for it, once, and merge on exactly the reasoning disposal
471	// uses. Same decision, made now.
472	//
473	// Bounded and cheap because it only runs where the arm ALREADY failed (or was
474	// skipped as doomed): a healthy member arms and returns without waiting at all.
475	//
476	// TWO BOUNDS, because the poll waits on two different things and one number
477	// answered both wrongly (#1026). Nothing VISIBLE is the short case: a dispatch
478	// that has registered no run is a dispatch that may never register one, so
479	// waiting long buys nothing. A run we can see EXECUTING is the opposite — the
480	// evidence is on its way and the only question is whether the prework budget
481	// outlasts it. Collapsed into one 180s bound, a member whose slowest check took
482	// 3m29s had its green PR abandoned 26s short every cycle and landed a day late
483	// by the next cycle's disposal, which is exactly the offset #649 closed.
484	//
485	// The in-flight bound is ceilinged by the PREWORK budget it spends, not by what
486	// CI might take: the shortest `prework_timeout` among the tasks that land their
487	// own PR is 600s, and the poll is the tail of a prework that has already
488	// converged a tree and opened a PR. 300s leaves that preamble its room. A member
489	// whose CI outruns even this still lands next cycle — but now it SAYS so
490	// (failureSummary below), where before the give-up was indistinguishable from a
491	// red repo.
492	export const LAND_TIMEOUT_MS = 180_000;
493	export const LAND_INFLIGHT_TIMEOUT_MS = 300_000;
494	export const LAND_POLL_MS = 5_000;
495	
496	// What one poll of the head sha's runs says to do: `merge`, `poll` again, or
497	// `give-up` and leave the PR standing.
498	//
499	// It NEVER closes. A cycle's disposal may close a PR because that PR is LAST
500	// cycle's and this cycle is about to re-cut it; here the PR IS this run's
501	// delivery, and closing it would throw away the work that just ran. A red member
502	// therefore leaves its PR open for the next run (or a human) to dispose of.
503	// `expected` is how many verification runs THIS delivery dispatched on the head
504	// seconds ago. Fewer visible runs than that is the API still registering them —
505	// a POLL, never a verdict. Without it, the first read 0.3s after the dispatch
506	// found an empty list and judged "nothing will ever verify this", stranding
507	// seven members' green PRs in one forced fleet pass (2026-08-07).
508	export function landAttempt({
509	  delivery, runs, expected = 0, elapsedMs = 0,
510	  timeoutMs = LAND_TIMEOUT_MS, inflightTimeoutMs = LAND_INFLIGHT_TIMEOUT_MS,
511	}) {
512	  if ((runs ?? []).length < expected) return elapsedMs >= timeoutMs ? 'give-up' : 'poll';
513	  const disposition = pullDisposition({ delivery, runs });
514	  if (disposition === 'merge') return 'merge';
515	  // `wait` is precisely "a run is not `completed`" — the seen-it-executing case,
516	  // which gets the longer budget.
517	  if (disposition === 'wait') return elapsedMs >= inflightTimeoutMs ? 'give-up' : 'poll';
518	  return 'give-up';
519	}
